// The stage director's pacing rules, as contracts a solver checks rather than
// comments I have to believe.
//
// This is a separate file for a mechanical reason worth writing down: `milo
// prove` generates verification conditions for every function in a file that
// carries a contract, and `world.milo` is two thousand lines of gameplay. Proving
// it did not finish in five minutes, and the compile-time contract gate runs on
// every push. Keeping the provable decisions in their own small module makes the
// proof instant and costs nothing — the rules were pure functions anyway.
//
// The rolls arrive as parameters because randomness is not provable. Drawing them
// at the call site leaves behind a decision that is.
//
// Both functions were shaped for the old Fourier-Motzkin solver (removed; z3 is the only
// solver now), which decided a straight-line chain of up to three branches and gave up
// at four. `unlockedSquads` stays a clamp-and-offset rather than the five-arm staircase
// it reads as, and the rarity re-roll lives at the call site rather than in here;
// z3 would prove the longer forms too.

pub let SQUAD_DRONES: i64 = 0

// Which squad enters next.
//
// The second postcondition is the bug this game actually shipped: with no
// `intro` guard your very first encounter could roll wall-mounted turrets, which
// is an ambush at the point where you do not yet know what the game is. Delete
// the guard below and `milo prove` refutes it with a counterexample — `intro = 1,
// roll = 1, unlocked = 2, result = 1` — instead of it being found by playing.
//
// The first postcondition is the cheaper kind of safety: `runSquad` dispatches on
// this id, so "always a valid squad" is worth stating even though nothing has
// ever violated it.
pub fn pickSquad(intro: i64, unlocked: i64, roll: i64): i64
requires unlocked >= 1
requires roll >= 0 && roll < unlocked
ensures result >= 0 && result < unlocked
ensures!(intro > 0) || result == SQUAD_DRONES
{
    if intro > 0 {
        return SQUAD_DRONES // the opening is taught, never rolled
    }
    return roll
}

// How many squad types a stage may draw from: 4 at stage 1, one more each stage,
// capped at 8. One new kind per stage, in a fixed order, so the roster is learned
// rather than dumped.
pub fn unlockedSquads(stage: i64): i64
ensures result >= 1 && result <= 8
{
    if stage <= 1 {
        return 4
    }
    if stage >= 5 {
        return 8
    }
    return stage + 3
}
